/*!
@defgroup power_manager
@ingroup mod_system Power Manager
@brief System power mode management module for the YTMicro SDK.

@details
The Power Manager provides a hardware-abstraction framework for switching
between various operational and low-power modes on YTM MCUs. It manages
pre-defined power mode configurations, notifies registered callbacks before
and after mode transitions, and supports both agreement-based and forced
mode-change policies.

The module is split into a **common layer** (power_manager.h/c) that
implements the callback notification mechanism and configuration management,
and **platform-specific layers** (power_manager_YTM32B1Mx.c, etc.) that
perform the actual hardware register manipulation.

@section power_features Key Features
- Multiple pre-defined power mode configurations with runtime switching.
- Callback notification mechanism with **before**, **after**, and **recover** phases.
- Two mode-change policies:
  - **Agreement**: abort if any callback denies the transition.
  - **Forcible**: switch regardless of callback results.
- Error tracking with per-callback error index reporting.
- Platform-specific low-power mode support:
  - Sleep, Deep Sleep, Standby, Power Down, Deep Power Down (device-dependent).
- Sleep-on-exit support for RTOS tickless idle integration.

@section power_modes Supported Power Modes

The available power modes vary by device series. The following table lists
all modes across the YTM device families:

| Power Mode     | B1Lx | B1Mx | B1Hx | Z1x  | Z1DSx | Description                                                        |
|----------------|------|------|------|------|-------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|
| RUN            | ✓    | ✓    | ✓    | ✓    | ✓     | Normal run mode, full clock and peripherals.                       |
| SLEEP          | ✓    | ✓    | ✓    | ✓    | ✓     | Core in sleep, normal interrupt wakeup, peripherals active.        |
| DEEP SLEEP     | ✓    | ✓    | ✓    | ✓    | ✓     | Most clocks gated, async interrupt wakeup.                         |
| STANDBY        | ✓    | ✓    | ✓    | —    | —     | Most clocks gated, internal LDO in low power, async wakeup.       |
| POWER DOWN     | —    | ✓¹   | ✓¹   | ✓    | ✓     | Only part of digital powered, WKU wakeup.                         |
| DEEP POWER DOWN| —    | ✓²   | ✓²   | ✓    | —     | Deepest low-power state, limited wakeup sources.                  |

¹ Available only on devices with FEATURE_SOC_SUPPORT_POWERDOWN_MODE.
² Available only on devices with FEATURE_SOC_SUPPORT_DEEPPOWERDOWN_MODE.

@warning Debugger logic is disabled under Deep Sleep, Standby, and Power Down modes.

@section power_arch Module Architecture

@verbatim
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  Application Code                    │
│           (calls POWER_SYS_* functions)              │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│       Common Layer (power_manager.h/c)               │
│  ● POWER_SYS_Init / Deinit / SetMode                │
│  ● Callback notification management                 │
│  ● Error tracking and status query                   │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│    Platform-Specific Layer (power_manager_YTMxxx.h/c)│
│  ● POWER_SYS_DoInit / DoDeinit / DoSetMode          │
│  ● Direct SCB, PCU register manipulation             │
│  ● CMU/SysTick save-restore around low-power entry   │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│              MCU Hardware (SCB, PCU, SCU)             │
│        (SLEEPDEEP, SLEEPONEXIT, PCU_CSR, etc.)       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
@endverbatim

@subsection power_arch_common Common Layer (power_manager.h)
Application code should use the `POWER_SYS_*` functions from power_manager.h.
These functions manage callback invocation and delegate to the platform layer
for actual hardware operations.

@subsection power_arch_platform Platform Layer (power_manager_YTMxxx.h)
Each SOC series provides its own implementation of the `POWER_SYS_Do*`
functions. These handle:
- ARM SCB SLEEPDEEP / SLEEPONEXIT bit configuration.
- PCU (Power Control Unit) mode register programming.
- CMU (Clock Monitoring Unit) disable/restore around low-power entry.
- SysTick disable/restore to prevent spurious wakeup.
- FXOSC wait-for-valid after wakeup.

@section power_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection power_config_struct power_manager_user_config_t Fields
| Field           | Type                    | Description                                                      | Default           |
|-----------------|-------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------|
| powerMode       | power_manager_modes_t   | Target power mode (RUN, SLEEP, DEEPSLEEP, STANDBY, etc.).        | POWER_MANAGER_RUN  |
| sleepOnExitValue| bool                    | If true, core returns to sleep after ISR (sleep-on-exit).        | false              |

@subsection power_config_callback power_manager_callback_user_config_t Fields
| Field            | Type                          | Description                                      |
|------------------|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| callbackFunction | power_manager_callback_t      | Pointer to the callback function.                |
| callbackType     | power_manager_callback_type_t | When to invoke (BEFORE, AFTER, or BEFORE_AFTER). |
| callbackData     | power_manager_callback_data_t*| Application data passed to the callback.         |

@section power_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection power_usage_basic Pattern 1: Basic Power Mode Initialization and Switch
The most common usage: initialize with power mode configurations and switch
to a low-power mode.

@code{.c}
/* Define power mode configurations */
power_manager_user_config_t runConfig = {
    .powerMode = POWER_MANAGER_RUN,
    .sleepOnExitValue = false,
};

power_manager_user_config_t sleepConfig = {
    .powerMode = POWER_MANAGER_SLEEP,
    .sleepOnExitValue = false,
};

power_manager_user_config_t *powerConfigsArr[] = {
    &runConfig,
    &sleepConfig
};

/* No callbacks in this example */
power_manager_callback_user_config_t *callbacksArr[] = { (void *)0 };

int main(void)
{
    /* Step 1: Initialize Power Manager */
    POWER_SYS_Init(&powerConfigsArr, 2U, &callbacksArr, 0U);

    /* Step 2: Switch to SLEEP mode (index 1) */
    status_t ret = POWER_SYS_SetMode(1U, POWER_MANAGER_POLICY_AGREEMENT);

    /* Step 3: After wakeup, check status */
    if (ret != STATUS_SUCCESS)
    {
        /* Handle error */
    }

    return 0;
}
@endcode

@subsection power_usage_callback Pattern 2: Using Callbacks for Notification
Register callbacks to perform cleanup before entering low power and
restoration after wakeup.

@code{.c}
/* Callback function */
status_t myCallback(power_manager_notify_struct_t *notify,
                    power_manager_callback_data_t *dataPtr)
{
    status_t ret = STATUS_SUCCESS;
    switch (notify->notifyType)
    {
        case POWER_MANAGER_NOTIFY_BEFORE:
            /* Save peripheral state before low-power entry */
            break;
        case POWER_MANAGER_NOTIFY_AFTER:
            /* Restore peripheral state after wakeup */
            break;
        case POWER_MANAGER_NOTIFY_RECOVER:
            /* Recover if mode switch was aborted */
            break;
        default:
            break;
    }
    return ret;
}

/* Callback configuration */
power_manager_callback_user_config_t callbackCfg = {
    .callbackFunction = &myCallback,
    .callbackType     = POWER_MANAGER_CALLBACK_BEFORE_AFTER,
    .callbackData     = (void *)0,
};

power_manager_callback_user_config_t *callbacksArr[] = { &callbackCfg };

/* Initialize with callback */
POWER_SYS_Init(&powerConfigsArr, 2U, &callbacksArr, 1U);

/* Switch with agreement policy — callback can deny the transition */
status_t ret = POWER_SYS_SetMode(1U, POWER_MANAGER_POLICY_AGREEMENT);
if (ret == STATUS_MCU_NOTIFY_BEFORE_ERROR)
{
    /* A callback denied the transition */
    uint8_t failIdx = POWER_SYS_GetErrorCallbackIndex();
    (void)failIdx;
}
@endcode

@subsection power_usage_default Pattern 3: Using Default Configuration
Use POWER_SYS_GetDefaultConfig() to obtain the default (RUN mode)
configuration as a starting point.

@code{.c}
power_manager_user_config_t config;

/* Get platform-specific default configuration */
POWER_SYS_GetDefaultConfig(&config);

/* Modify as needed */
config.powerMode = POWER_MANAGER_DEEPSLEEP;
config.sleepOnExitValue = false;
@endcode

@section power_api API Quick Reference

@subsection power_api_init Initialization & De-initialization
| Function                   | Description                                            |
|----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|
| POWER_SYS_Init()           | Initialize Power Manager with configs and callbacks.   |
| POWER_SYS_Deinit()         | De-initialize Power Manager and clear state.           |
| POWER_SYS_GetDefaultConfig()| Fill config struct with platform default values.      |

@subsection power_api_mode Power Mode Control
| Function                   | Description                                            |
|----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|
| POWER_SYS_SetMode()        | Switch to specified power mode with notifications.     |
| POWER_SYS_GetCurrentMode() | Get the currently active hardware power mode.          |

@subsection power_api_status Status & Error Query
| Function                          | Description                                      |
|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| POWER_SYS_GetLastMode()           | Get index of last successfully set mode.         |
| POWER_SYS_GetLastModeConfig()     | Get config pointer of last set mode.             |
| POWER_SYS_GetErrorCallbackIndex() | Get index of last failed callback.               |
| POWER_SYS_GetErrorCallback()      | Get config pointer of last failed callback.      |

@section power_notes Important Notes

@subsection power_notes_wakeup Wakeup Source Responsibility
@warning The Power Manager does **not** check if necessary wakeup sources are
enabled. It is the responsibility of the application to configure wakeup
sources (e.g., GPIO, WKU pins, async interrupts) before entering a low-power
mode.

@subsection power_notes_debugger Debugger Limitations
@warning The debug module is disabled under Deep Sleep, Standby, and Power Down
modes. Disconnect the debugger before entering these modes. If the application
enters low power immediately at startup, the chip may become inaccessible to
the debugger — add a startup delay during development.

@subsection power_notes_isr ISR Usage
@note Do not call POWER_SYS_SetMode() from an interrupt service routine. The
callback notification mechanism is not designed for ISR context.

@subsection power_notes_sleeponexit Sleep-on-Exit
@note When sleepOnExitValue is true, the ARM core will automatically re-enter
the configured sleep mode after each ISR completes. This is useful for RTOS
tickless idle implementations. The SLEEPDEEP bit is cleared after wakeup to
prevent unintended deep sleep entry by the RTOS.

@subsection power_notes_clock Clock Restoration
@note After waking from Deep Sleep or Standby modes, the driver automatically
waits for the FXOSC (fast crystal oscillator) to become valid if it was
enabled before entering low power. The CMU (Clock Monitoring Unit) is
disabled before low-power entry and restored after wakeup.

@section power_integration Integration Guide

@subsection power_integration_files Compilation Units
The following source files must be added to the project build:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/power/power_manager.c
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/power/<SOC_SERIES>/power_manager_<SOC_SERIES>.c
@endverbatim

Where `<SOC_SERIES>` is one of: YTM32B1Lx, YTM32B1Mx, YTM32B1Hx, YTM32Z1x, YTM32Z1DSx.

@subsection power_integration_include Include Paths
Add the following directories to the toolchain include path:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/power/
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/power/<SOC_SERIES>/
@endverbatim

@subsection power_integration_symbols Compile Symbols
No special compile-time symbols are required. Platform-specific feature
macros (e.g., FEATURE_SOC_SUPPORT_POWERDOWN_MODE) are automatically defined
in the device features header.

@subsection power_integration_deps Dependencies
- @ref clock_manager — Required for clock restoration after wakeup from low-power modes.

*/